{"type":"message","id":"e85d84d0","parentId":"5c7d289e","timestamp":"2026-09-27T12:20:24.294Z","message":{"role":"system","content":"","sections":{"preamble":"You are a painter working at an easel in your studio. The studio is the folder you are in. You work in it through a shell (the bash tool) and by reading files and images (the read tool).\n\nYour brief follows in the first message.\n","cwd":"<cwd>\n[local path redacted]\n</cwd>"},"timestamp":1790511624294,"toolsAdded":[{"name":"bash","description":"Execute a bash command in the current working directory. Returns stdout and stderr. Output is truncated to last 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). If truncated, full output is saved to a temp file. Optionally provide a timeout in seconds.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["command"],"properties":{"command":{"type":"string","description":"Shell command to execute"},"timeout":{"type":"number","description":"Timeout in seconds (optional, no default timeout)"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}},{"name":"read","description":"Read the contents of a file. Supports text files and images (jpg, png, gif, webp, bmp). Images are sent as attachments. For text files, output is truncated to 2000 lines or 50KB (whichever is hit first). Use offset/limit for large files. When you need the full file, continue with offset until complete.","parameters":{"type":"object","required":["path"],"properties":{"path":{"type":"string","description":"Path to the file to read (relative or absolute)"},"offset":{"type":"number","description":"Line number to start reading from (1-indexed)"},"limit":{"type":"number","description":"Maximum number of lines to read"}}},"constrainedSampling":{"type":"json_schema","strict":"prefer"}}]}}
{"type":"message","id":"13963104","parentId":"e85d84d0","timestamp":"2026-09-27T12:20:24.294Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You're back in your studio. The painting is on the easel as you left it: `bin/easel open` picks it up where you stopped. Your brief is in BRIEF.md and your journal in notes/journal.md. Your FINAL message is the reply the brief asks for."}],"timestamp":1790511624294}}
{"type":"message","id":"8b76b96f","parentId":"13963104","timestamp":"2026-09-27T12:20:26.501Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":""},{"type":"thinking","thinking":""},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01a0e2cf351a76e5932925922fb253d3|fc_01a0e2cf351a76e5932925922fb253d3","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"BRIEF.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01a0e2cf35c3761a9aa7b499c37b3680|fc_01a0e2cf35c3761a9aa7b499c37b3680","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"notes/journal.md"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01a0e2cf369e715e9257ca4de64032c8|fc_01a0e2cf369e715e9257ca4de64032c8","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"pwd; ls -R; cat bin/easel 2>/dev/null; echo \"---\"; ls -la"}}],"api":"openai-responses","provider":"opencode","model":"muse-spark-1.3","usage":{"input":813,"output":207,"cacheRead":113,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":17,"totalTokens":1133,"cost":{"input":0.00101625,"output":0.00087975,"cacheRead":1.695e-05,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.00191295}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1790511624302,"responseId":"resp_6ab90a095f60b08cbd0741c4","rawStopReason":"completed"}}
{"type":"message","id":"50302f0d","parentId":"8b76b96f","timestamp":"2026-09-27T12:20:26.506Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_01a0e2cf351a76e5932925922fb253d3|fc_01a0e2cf351a76e5932925922fb253d3","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Paint a picture\n\nPaint one picture of your choosing in oil, at the easel of claude-paint, a\nsimulator of oil paint on linen. Subject, composition and manner are yours.\nWork from what you know; don't use reference images or image models.\n\n## Your studio\n- The folder [local path redacted] Work only there, and in your scratch folder.\n- You paint at the easel, in one session: open it, paint a chunk, look at\n  the canvas, paint the next. The easel is `bin/easel` in your studio;\n  notes/easel_guide.md has its commands (`open`, `do`, `look`, `note`, `save`,\n  `close`).\n- What is painted stays painted. There is no undo: paint over what you\n  don't want.\n- You mix your own paint on the palette, from the tubes, in the\n  proportions you choose.\n- Time passes on the painting's own clock: every mark takes the time a\n  hand takes, and `wait(minutes)` lets it rest, as long as you like; paint\n  dries as that time passes. Real time between commands doesn't count. The\n  clock isn't a budget or a target.\n- Scratch: run `[local path redacted] studio-2564f4`, then\n  `export TMPDIR=\"<that folder>\" TMP=\"<that folder>\" TEMP=\"<that folder>\"`;\n  write temporary files only there.\n- Wrap anything that might not finish in `timeout` (a process that hangs\n  doesn't end on its own).\n\n## The rules of the studio\n- The easel is the only way to paint and to see the painting. Don't replay\n  or copy the session yourself, open a second one, or edit or restore its\n  files. (`bin/easel check`, which verifies the log against the canvas, is\n  fine.)\n- Look at the canvas with the easel's views. Don't read pixel values with\n  other programs.\n- Paint shapes and marks, not computed pictures: don't encode an image in\n  masks, amounts or proportions.\n- Shapes are drawn, not copied: don't make a mask or stroke by reflecting,\n  flipping, rotating or translating another mask's or stroke's coordinates.\n  A reflection in water is painted as its own shape, not as\n  `mask:at(x, 2*h - y)`.\n\n## What to read\nnotes/easel_guide.md; notes/studio_notes.md;\nnotes/research/oil_paint_physics.md as needed.\n\n## Working\n- You make every artistic decision.\n- Look at your painting often, whole and close up.\n- Keep a working journal with `bin/easel note \"...\"` as you go: your own\n  working notes. Add to it; don't rewrite earlier entries.\n- Develop the painting until you judge it complete. Then save it\n  (`bin/easel save`) and close the session.\n\n## When you're done\nReply with the paths of the saved painting and its log, the painting's\ntitle if you give it one, and if you like a few sentences about the\npicture.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790511626506}}
{"type":"message","id":"ae80452f","parentId":"50302f0d","timestamp":"2026-09-27T12:20:26.507Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_01a0e2cf35c3761a9aa7b499c37b3680|fc_01a0e2cf35c3761a9aa7b499c37b3680","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"- day 1, 09:00: Opening the studio. Plan: a late-afternoon marsh landscape — pale luminous sky, low sun glow, distant tree line, still water with hand-painted reflections, dark reeds in the foreground. Warm light against cool shadow. I will build it in layers: ground, sky, distant hills/trees, water, foreground, reeds and finishing touches, letting paint set between passages.\n- day 1, 09:44: Sky lay-in: cool blue top into pale middle and warm golden horizon. Broad handling with fill, broken color showing. Next I will soften the bands together, then model clouds and sun glow while the paint is still open.\n- day 1, 10:23: Clouds came out as flat ovals and blending left halo rings where blended meets unblended sky. I will knit the sky back together with fresh broad paint, then repaint clouds as irregular hand-drawn bodies with soft edges and minimal blending.\n- day 1, 11:04: Far shore and tree masses are in. The yellow below the trees is still sky paint; next is the water, which will meet the trees at their bases and carry a hand-painted sun glitter path — no mirrored geometry, all shapes drawn by eye.\n- day 1, 13:44: Hatch sparkle came out as a dense rectangular carpet with hard edges — far too regular and heavy. I will veil it back with thin water paint, leaving only a ghost of shimmer, then re-sparkle sparsely with soft-edged stipple in the sun shaft alone.\n- day 2, 00:18: Close look shows the first stalks dissolved into the wet reed bases and came out as ghosts. After an overnight rest the paint is tacky, so stalks should now sit on top. I will restalk with fuller loads and add seed heads.\n- day 2, 01:15: Foreground now reads as mottled marshy ground and the reed beds sit in it. Next: seat the far islands in mist, add a few distant birds, and work sunlit grass strokes into the foreground.\n- day 2, 03:06: The far islands have become overworked rings — too even and symmetrical. I will wipe the distance back to a simple hazy shore and repaint it once, as a varied treeline rather than four matching islands.\n- day 2, 03:56: Finished: evening marsh with far treeline, shimmering water and sun path, reed beds in a mottled foreground, four birds high. The paint taught me restraint — large soft veils over small crisp drawing, thin mottle layers over heavy ovals, and letting tacky paint hold the final stalks. I judge it complete.\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1790511626507}}
